---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const themes = [
  {
    title: 'Clarity',
    body: 'Seeing things as they are—not as we were taught, promised, or pressured to see them—is the beginning of every good decision.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Reinvention',
    body: 'Starting over is not failure. Sometimes it is the first honest decision a woman can make for herself.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Financial Awareness',
    body: 'Money is not a dirty word. Silence around it can be devastating. Women deserve full, honest knowledge of their financial lives.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Friendship',
    body: 'The women worth keeping are the ones who can comfort you without helping you hide.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Courage',
    body: 'Not the loud kind, but the quiet, daily courage to name what is true, leave what no longer fits, and begin again.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Moral Ambiguity',
    body: 'There is rarely one clean, correct way to navigate a difficult situation. Understanding a choice is not the same as excusing it.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Institutional Accountability',
    body: 'Institutions have responsibilities beyond protecting themselves. Passing a problem along does not resolve it—and institutional silence can have consequences far beyond the institution itself.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  active="home"
  title="Monica Messer — Author of The Family Narrative"
  description="Monica Messer writes character-driven fiction about women, families, and institutions. Her debut novel, The Family Narrative, is complete at approximately 72,000 words."
>
  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container hero-grid">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">Monica Messer</p>
        <h1>Stories about marriage, motherhood, money, and the family narratives and legacies that shape us.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Monica Messer writes character-driven fiction about women, families, and institutions—and the
          moments when accepting the familiar story is no longer enough.
        </p>
        <div class="btn-group">
          <a href="/the-family-narrative/" class="btn btn-primary">The Family Narrative</a>
          <a href="/read/" class="btn btn-outline">Read Chapter One</a>
          <a href="/read/#listen" class="btn btn-outline">Listen to Chapter One</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-media">
        <img src="/assets/book-cover.png" alt="The Family Narrative by Monica Messer" class="cover-img" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- FEATURED BOOK -->
  <section class="bg-sand">
    <div class="container split">
      <div class="split-media">
        <img src="/assets/book-cover.png" alt="The Family Narrative by Monica Messer" class="cover-img" />
      </div>
      <div class="split-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">The Family Narrative</p>
        <h2>Every family has a story.</h2>
        <p>
          Frances Stanton has spent decades building a life that works. A respected professor. A beautiful
          home in Los Gatos. Two sons nearly grown. A husband whose family name opens doors at the
          university, in the community, and in every room that matters.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then, over lunch on her fifty-fourth birthday, Frances overhears a conversation that makes
          her question everything she thought she knew about her marriage.
        </p>
        <p>
          At first, she believes the betrayal is an affair. But as the truth surfaces, Frances discovers
          something far larger: hidden debt, addiction, family complicity, institutional silence, and a
          carefully protected version of the Stanton story that everyone around her seemed to understand
          before she did.
        </p>
        <p>
          When her husband files for divorce before she has even caught up to the truth, Frances is forced
          to confront not only what he has hidden, but what she allowed herself not to examine—especially
          the financial life she trusted him to manage.
        </p>
        <p>
          To move forward, she must learn how to protect herself without losing her compassion, how to
          tell her sons the truth without asking them to inherit its burden, and how to decide what parts
          of the Stanton story are worth carrying forward.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>The Family Narrative</em> is a novel about marriage, money, reputation, and the stories
          families preserve—until someone decides the truth matters more.
        </p>
        <p class="lede" style="margin-top:1.5em;">
          What do we owe the stories we inherit—and what do we choose to pass on?
        </p>
        <div class="btn-group">
          <a href="/the-family-narrative/" class="btn btn-primary">The Family Narrative</a>
          <a href="/read/" class="btn btn-outline">Read Chapter One</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CORE THEMES -->
  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head center">
        <p class="eyebrow">Core Themes in Monica's Writing</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid-cards values-cards">
        {themes.map((t) => (
          <div class="theme-card">
            <h3>{t.title}</h3>
            <p>{t.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- WHAT I'M WRITING NEXT (teaser) -->
  <section class="bg-sand2">
    <div class="container narrow center">
      <p class="eyebrow">What I'm Writing Next</p>
      <p class="lede measure">
        The questions at the heart of <em>The Family Narrative</em> continue into Monica's next novels:
        What do we owe one another when we recognize something is wrong? When does looking away become
        complicity? And what, intentionally or not, do we pass on to the people who come after us?
      </p>
      <div class="btn-group center">
        <a href="/writing/" class="btn btn-primary">Explore What's Next</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
